Q.kpo_last_summary = "The spreadsheet improves while implementation and later-payment risk worsen.";
Q.news_headline = "The budget counts KPO statutes before institutions can deliver them";
!}

Legal adoption is entered in the delivery column. The spreadsheet balances, the
ordinary budget survives December without a public argument, and every one of
those three statements is, in the narrowest sense, true. The statutes were
adopted. The column is for adopted statutes.

The implementation those statutes were adopted in order to trigger has not
begun anywhere. We have moved the problem twelve months down the road, where it
will be bigger, and where the people currently defending these figures will
mostly have other jobs.

[? if kpo_ledger_public = 1: February's ledger makes this considerably worse than it needed to be. The dates are published, in our own columns, under our own names; the gap between what the ledger says is due and what the budget now counts as delivered is a table anybody can build in an afternoon, and by Thursday three journalists have.?]
[? if kpo_ledger_public = 0: With no published ledger there is no document to contradict, and the manoeuvre passes almost unnoticed. That is the argument for having never published one, and it is being made, this week, by people who voted against publishing it.?]

- @kpo_result: Count the adoption

@budget_gate_opposition
title: Separate KPO grants from the government's unfunded promises
subtitle: One table, two documents, and the number of promises being counted twice.
view-if: left_in_government = 0
on-arrival: {!
Q.kpo_public_credit += Q.kpo_opposition_file ? 3 : 2;
Q.media_capacity += 2;
Q.public_trust += 2;
Q.ko_relation -= 3;
Q.kpo_scrutiny += 1;
Q.kpo_last_credit_change = Q.kpo_opposition_file ? 3 : 2;
Q.kpo_last_delivery_change = 0;
Q.kpo_last_summary = "The shadow budget exposes double counting without taking responsibility for state spending.";
Q.news_headline = "The Left marks every KPO grant in the opposition budget ledger";
!}

We take the government's budget apart on exactly one point: which promises are
funded from European grants, and which of them appear in both the grant column
and the domestic column. It requires no cabinet authority whatsoever. It
requires the patience to reconcile two documents that the government has very
deliberately published a fortnight apart and in different formats.

[? if kpo_opposition_file = 1: Our monitoring team has been holding one half of that reconciliation since February, so the work takes four days rather than four weeks and lands while the budget is still being voted rather than after.?]
[? if kpo_opposition_file = 0: With no standing team, three people do it over two weekends and it lands eight days after the vote — accurate, unanswerable, and a news cycle too late to change a single line.?]

**[+ finance_minister +]** dislikes it because the answer is embarrassing and
almost impossible to dispute in public. What it costs us is that it commits us
to nothing: we have exposed the arithmetic without ever having to say which of
the double-counted promises a Lewica budget would drop, and that is the first
question we will be asked if we are ever in the room.

- @kpo_result: Publish the separation


